page cmd_worktree {
  title = "vmlab worktree"
  chapter_header "vmlab worktree" {
    kicker       = "Commands · reference"
    reading_time = "6 min read"
    updated      = "2026-10-28"
    version      = "vmlab 0.31"
  }

  p <<DOC
    `vmlab worktree` freezes the main checkout's lab for
    [worktree labs](guide_worktrees) to clone their machines from, thaws it,
    and reports where the family stands. `freeze` and `thaw` run in the main
    checkout and go to its lab daemon, as the snapshot verbs do. `status`
    reads the files alone, so it answers in the main checkout or any linked
    worktree with no daemon running.
  DOC

  code sh { source = "vmlab worktree <COMMAND>" }

  table {
    rows:
      | "Subcommand" | "Meaning" |
      | "`freeze`" | "Freeze the stopped main lab at an offline snapshot every machine has, for linked git worktrees to clone their machines from." |
      | "`thaw`" | "Fold the freeze back into the stopped main lab's disks and unlock its earlier snapshots." |
      | "`status`" | "Each generation of the main lab's freeze, its snapshot, and the worktrees whose disks back onto it." |
      | "`-h`, `--help`" | "Print help." |
  }

  p <<DOC
    A lab daemon decides [worktree isolation](guide_worktrees) when it
    starts, and `freeze` and `thaw` run in a daemon that started before
    them. The next `up`, `pull`, `vm start` or `vm restart` in the main
    checkout replaces that daemon first, as it does after an edit to
    `vmlab.wcl` (see [vmlab up](cmd_up)). Unlike an edit, this cannot be
    reloaded: it refuses while a machine runs.
  DOC

  h2 "vmlab worktree freeze"

  code sh { source = "vmlab worktree freeze [OPTIONS] <SNAPSHOT>" }

  table {
    rows:
      | "Option" | "Meaning" |
      | "`<SNAPSHOT>`" | "The offline snapshot to freeze at." |
      | "`--json`" | "Emit the raw JSON reply instead of a report." |
      | "`-h`, `--help`" | "Print help." |
  }

  p <<DOC
    Freezes every machine of the lab at `SNAPSHOT` as the next
    **generation**, `1` for the first freeze. Each disk is rewound to the
    snapshot, moved into `.vmlab/freeze/<n>/<machine>/` and made read-only,
    and the machine's disk becomes an overlay on it that holds the state the
    lab had, so the main lab carries on unchanged. The machine's
    `OVMF_VARS.fd`, `tpm-state/`, `clone-source`, `firstboot.done` and state record, and the
    lab's named volumes, are copied into the generation. `.vmlab/worktree.json`
    is written last, naming the generation and the snapshot; its presence is
    what makes the lab frozen. A failure at any step undoes every step
    before it, and the error says so.
  DOC

  p <<DOC
    The overlay is written by copying the disk (`cp --reflink=auto`, free on
    a filesystem with reflinks) and rebasing an empty overlay onto the
    frozen file with a safe `qemu-img rebase`, which writes only the
    clusters that differ from the snapshot. Without reflinks each copy is a
    full one, and every disk's copy stays until the whole freeze has
    committed.
  DOC

  p <<DOC
    `SNAPSHOT` becomes the **freeze point**, and every other snapshot of the
    lab is **locked** in this generation: see [vmlab snapshot](cmd_snapshot).
    Freezing again freezes the main lab's current overlays as generation
    `n+1`, backed by generation `n`, and the previous freeze point is locked
    with the rest. On success it prints:
  DOC

  code text { source = <<'SRC'
froze generation 1 at "provisioned": dc01, web
snapshots taken before it are locked until `vmlab worktree thaw`; linked worktrees now clone these machines on `vmlab up`
SRC
  }

  p "Then a `WARNING:` line for each leftover file it could not clean up. It refuses, and changes nothing, when:"

  list {
    li "the lab is in a linked git worktree; only the main checkout's lab freezes,"
    li "the lab declares no machines,"
    li "a machine is running (`vmlab down` first),"
    li "a machine has no snapshot `SNAPSHOT`, or has never been built,"
    li "the snapshot was taken online,"
    li "the snapshot is itself locked in an earlier generation (take a new offline snapshot),"
    li "a disk lacks the internal snapshot the lab's record says it holds,"
    li "a disk still holds the temporary snapshot an interrupted freeze or thaw left behind,"
    li "a disk names its backing file by a relative path, which the move would break."
  }

  h2 "vmlab worktree thaw"

  code sh { source = "vmlab worktree thaw [OPTIONS]" }

  table {
    rows:
      | "Option" | "Meaning" |
      | "`--drop-online-snapshots`" | "Give up the online snapshots taken since a freeze, whose machine state cannot be carried into the thawed disks, rather than refuse." |
      | "`--json`" | "Emit the raw JSON reply instead of a report." |
      | "`-h`, `--help`" | "Print help." |
  }

  p <<DOC
    Collapses each disk's chain back into its oldest frozen file, which
    becomes the machine's disk again. Every layer above it is committed down
    with `qemu-img commit`, and the snapshots each layer holds are carried
    across one by one, so offline snapshots taken after a freeze survive.
    The main lab keeps its current state. `.vmlab/freeze/` and
    `worktree.json` are removed and every snapshot is unlocked. On success it
    prints:
  DOC

  code text { source = <<'SRC'
thawed: the freeze is gone and every snapshot is unlocked
SRC
  }

  p <<DOC
    With `--drop-online-snapshots` it adds `dropped snapshot "<name>" of
    <machine>` for each snapshot it gave up, then any `WARNING:` lines. It
    refuses when:
  DOC

  list {
    li "the lab is in a linked git worktree; only the main checkout's lab thaws,"
    li "the lab is not frozen,"
    li "any worktree lab's disk backs onto any generation. The refusal names each worktree and its generations; run `vmlab destroy` in each to release it,"
    li "a machine is running (`vmlab down` first),"
    li "a machine holds online snapshots stored above its oldest generation, unless `--drop-online-snapshots` is given. The refusal names them,"
    li "a disk's chain is not the shape a freeze leaves: a temporary snapshot left by an interrupted freeze, a snapshot name stored twice in one chain, or a generation that does not hold its own freeze point."
  }

  h2 "vmlab worktree status"

  code sh { source = "vmlab worktree status [OPTIONS]" }

  table {
    rows:
      | "Option" | "Meaning" |
      | "`--json`" | "Emit the raw JSON instead of a table." |
      | "`-h`, `--help`" | "Print help." |
  }

  p <<DOC
    Prints the main checkout, then `this worktree:` when run in a linked
    worktree, then one row per generation: `GENERATION` (the newest marked
    `(newest)`), `SNAPSHOT`, `MACHINES` and `WORKTREES`, the linked worktree
    labs whose disks back onto it, or `-`. It reads every worktree disk's
    backing chain directly, so it counts worktree labs whose daemons are not
    running. An unfrozen lab prints `not frozen` with the command that
    freezes it. Generations on disk with no `worktree.json` add a warning
    that worktree labs do not clone from them.
  DOC

  h2 "Examples"

  p "Freeze a provisioned lab and bring it back up under isolation:"
  code sh { source = <<'SRC'
vmlab down
vmlab snapshot create provisioned
vmlab worktree freeze provisioned
vmlab up
SRC
  }

  p "See which worktrees still hold the freeze:"
  code sh { source = "vmlab worktree status" }

  code text { source = <<'SRC'
main checkout: /home/wil/src/app
GENERATION  SNAPSHOT     MACHINES   WORKTREES
1           provisioned  dc01, web  /home/wil/src/feat-x
2 (newest)  upgraded     dc01, web  -
SRC
  }

  p "Thaw, giving up the online snapshots taken since the freeze:"
  code sh { source = "vmlab worktree thaw --drop-online-snapshots" }

  h2 "Exit status"

  p <<DOC
    Exit status is 0 on success, and 0 for `status` on an unfrozen lab.
    Every refusal listed above exits 5 (`conflict`). A step that failed and
    was rolled back, an invalid snapshot name, or a daemon that cannot be
    reached exits 1 (`failed`). A usage error exits 2.
  DOC
}
